      "sources": [
        "NYC Open Data — MapPLUTO (64uk-42ks)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "observation": "The zoning designation is M1-4/R7-2, a manufacturing district with a residential overlay. This mixed designation permits residential development as-of-right under the R7-2 component (max residential FAR 3.44), which is a material positive versus a pure M- district. However, the manufacturing base district introduces complexity around use conflicts and conversion requirements.",
      "sources": [
        "NYC Open Data — MapPLUTO (64uk-42ks)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "observation": "Special district G is coded on this parcel (per the zoning source). Special districts modify what can be built as-of-right; the specific rules, restrictions, or programs associated with district G are not supplied by the data and cannot be inferred. This is a material entitlement uncertainty — the district code must be resolved against its actual regulations before development rights can be confirmed.",
      "sources": [
        "NYC Open Data — MapPLUTO (64uk-42ks)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "observation": "Building class G9 and land use code 7 indicate a garage or storage-type industrial structure built in 1973 with 0 residential units. The existing improvement is low-value relative to the lot, consistent with a site where the land — not the structure — drives value. Demolition or adaptive reuse is the likely path.",
      "sources": [
        "NYC Open Data — MapPLUTO (64uk-42ks)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "observation": "The site is not a federal Opportunity Zone, meaning the 10-year capital-gains basis step-up incentive is unavailable. This is neutral — not a negative — but removes a tax-structuring lever that would otherwise attract certain capital-gains investors to a long-horizon redevelopment play.",
      "sources": [
        "IRS/Treasury QOZ designations (public ArcGIS mirror)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "observation": "The property is not an HPD-registered multiple dwelling, with no registered owner, no HPD violations, and no Speculation Watch List flag. This means there is no tenant-displacement regulatory exposure and no operator-conduct risk from the HPD record — a clean regulatory slate from a housing-enforcement standpoint.",
      "sources": [
        "NYC Open Data — HPD registrations (tesw-yqqr), contacts (feu5-w2e2), Speculation Watch List (adax-9mit)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "minority_signals": [
    {
      "signal": "Max residential FAR of 3.44 under the R7-2 overlay on a 120,793 sq ft lot implies a theoretical as-of-right residential buildout of ~415,000 sq ft — an enormous value unlock that could argue for a more aggressive 'buy' verdict.",
      "note": "Special district G's as-of-right modifications are unresolved from the data alone. Until the district's specific constraints are confirmed, the full FAR envelope cannot be treated as deliverable, and 'wait' is the appropriate posture pending entitlement diligence."
    }
  ]
}

